<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1990s: Indurain, Pantani and Riis, integrated shifters, aluminium, carbon, EPO and the Festina affair."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1990-1999/galibier-1993.jpg"/><title>When shifting moved to the handlebars · 1990–1999 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1990-1999/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1970-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1990–1999</p><h1>When shifting moved<br/><em>to the handlebars</em></h1></div><p>Riders could change gear without letting go of the bars. Aluminium, carbon and deep rims changed the bicycle, while EPO changed the sport in a much darker way.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${images}/galibier-1993.jpg`} alt="Miguel Indurain in yellow in a small group on the Galibier during the 1993 Tour"/><figcaption>Álvaro Mejía, Miguel Indurain, Tony Rominger, Andy Hampsten and Zenon Jaskuła on the Galibier in 1993. Photo: ta_do · CC BY-SA 2.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">One hand stays on the bars</p><p>The most visible change sat in front of the rider. The lever left the down tube and joined the brake lever, allowing shifts during a sprint or while climbing out of the saddle.</p></section><section class="era-facts decade1970-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Five years with the same man</p><h2>Indurain won back the time</h2></div><div><p>Miguel Indurain won the Tour every year from 1991 to 1995, usually building his lead in long time trials and defending it in the mountains. It was less theatrical than a summit attack, but difficult to answer.</p><p>His run ended in 1996 when Bjarne Riis attacked repeatedly on Hautacam. Riis won the Tour, but his later admission of EPO use — including in 1996 — cannot be separated from the result.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">A modern cockpit</p><h2>STI, Ergopower and more sprockets</h2><p>Shimano introduced Dura-Ace Dual Control in 1990. Campagnolo followed with Ergopower, and handlebar shifting became the new normal. Indexed systems and shaped teeth improved changes, while top groups grew from eight to nine rear sprockets.</p></div><aside><span>STI</span><span>Ergopower</span><span>8–9 sprockets</span><span>Deep rims</span><span>Aluminium</span><span>Carbon</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">Changing materials</p><h2>Steel was no longer the default</h2></div><div><p>Large aluminium tubes gave frames a more angular appearance. Carbon progressed from joined tubes to more unified structures. Campagnolo’s 1992 Shamal made the factory-built deep-section wheel an obvious part of road racing.</p><p>Time-trial bicycles became highly specialised. At Disneyland Paris in 1997, Riis suffered mechanical trouble, dismounted and threw his expensive machine into the grass. Modern equipment could still receive a very simple response.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>Mont Ventoux · 1994</span><h2>The big man made it over first</h2><p>The 194-centimetre domestique Eros Poli began Ventoux with more than 20 minutes in hand. He lost most of it climbing, but enough remained for victory in Carpentras.</p></div><div><span>Giro and Tour · 1998</span><h2>Pantani attacked where Indurain waited</h2><p>Marco Pantani sought decisions on steep climbs and won both the Giro and Tour in 1998. No male rider has repeated the double since.</p></div></section>
<section class="era-gallery"><figure class="gallery-tall"><img src={`${images}/pantani-1997.jpg`} alt="Marco Pantani climbing during the 1997 Tour"/><figcaption>Pantani on Alpe d’Huez in 1997. Photo: Hein Ciere · CC BY 3.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/riis-hautacam-1996.jpg`} alt="Bjarne Riis ahead of Miguel Indurain on Hautacam in 1996"/><figcaption>Riis ahead of Indurain. Photo: Marco Ullrich · CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/grande-boucle-feminine-1999.jpg`} alt="Women riders in the 1999 Grande Boucle féminine"/><figcaption>The 1999 Grande Boucle féminine. Photo: Cjp24 · CC BY-SA 3.0.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Energy by numbers</p><h2>Food acquired a plan</h2></div><div><p>Teams worked more precisely with carbohydrate, fluids and recovery. Heart-rate monitors and laboratory tests produced more data. Professionalisation improved load management, but also enabled doping to be organised with the same attention to schedules and measurement.</p><p>The peloton was not uniform, and one substance cannot explain every result. Yet EPO increased the blood’s oxygen-carrying capacity, while no direct test could detect it.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Tour de France · 1998</p><h2>When the Festina affair opened the car door</h2></div><div><p>Festina soigneur Willy Voet’s car was stopped with large quantities of EPO, growth hormone and other drugs. Investigations, arrests and the team’s expulsion followed. Riders protested and stage 17 was neutralised.</p><p>Only 96 of 189 starters reached Paris. Pantani won, but 1998 was remembered for the system exposed behind the race. The affair also helped prompt the creation of WADA in 1999.</p></div></section>
